.TH smi-pki-export-pkcs12 "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-export-pkcs12 \- Bundle a private key and its certificate into a PKCS#12 file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-export-pkcs12
.I PRIV_KEY_FILE CERT_FILE P12_FILE FRIENDLY_NAME
.RI [ CA_CERT_FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Writes \fIP12_FILE\fR, a PKCS#12 bundle of \fIPRIV_KEY_FILE\fR and \fICERT_FILE\fR under the alias
\fIFRIENDLY_NAME\fR, mode \fB600\fR.
PKCS#12 is the single file format that browsers, Java keystores, .NET and many clients import;
the PEM key and certificate are separate files.
.PP
\fICA_CERT_FILE\fR, when given, is added to the bundle as the chain, so a client also presents the
Intermediate CA. Give the Intermediate CA certificate itself, not a chain file that already
contains the client certificate, or the client certificate ends up in the bundle twice.
.PP
The export password is taken from
.SM SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD
when that variable is set, never from the command line, where the process list would show it to
every user on the machine.
Without the variable OpenSSL asks for the password on the terminal; with no terminal the command
fails and writes no file.
.PP
The file is written with the OpenSSL 3 defaults, AES-256-CBC and PBKDF2. Very old clients that
cannot read those need a bundle made with the OpenSSL
.B -legacy
option.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD
Export password of the PKCS#12 file. Unset means asking on the terminal.
.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
On the client side, from the key it created and the certificate it received:
.RS
.nf
SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD=secret smi-pki-export-pkcs12 \\
    acme-billing.client.priv.key acme-billing.client.crt \\
    acme-billing.client.p12 acme-billing gintra.intermediate.crt
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smi-pki-export-client-pkcs12 (1),
.BR smi-pki-do-client-cert-request (1),
.BR smi-pki-create-client-cert (1),
.BR openssl (1)
